\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Positive Gaussian laws and covariance}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{A positive quadratic action weights large fields less heavily.
Does its integral over an infinite field space converge, and what
expectations does the normalized weight assign? The
[alternating four-cycle calculation](fdip-0037) answers both questions
explicitly: its four precision eigenvalues determine the normalizer
and the correlation between opposite vertices. The
[reference volume](fdip-003U) is fixed before this weight is chosen.}

\p{A [positive quadratic action](fdip-0004) on a finite real
inner-product space has a [finite Gaussian normalizer](fdip-002T).
[Absolute polynomial moments](fdip-002W) make its expectation a
[polynomial state](fdip-002X), and the [covariance theorem](fdip-000B)
identifies inverse precision as its covariance operator. Zero modes
require the separate constraints used for [massless laws](fdip-003V).
Averaging or fixing some fields will later give the distinct
[marginal](fdip-000E) and [conditional](fdip-003A) laws.}

